            <ArticleTitle>Altered interpretation of neck proprioceptive signals in persons with subclinical recurrent neck pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>426-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2340/16501977-0189</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate whether subjects with subclinical recurrent neck pain have an altered interpretation of neck proprioceptive signals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A comparative group design.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twelve subjects with recurrent neck pain and 12 control subjects.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The shoulder, head, trunk and whole-body position were measured under the following conditions: active and passive elevation and depression of the right shoulder and vibration of the trapezius muscle.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During passive shoulder movements both groups moved their head in the opposite direction (p&lt;0.05). During passive elevation subjects with recurrent neck pain showed a significant over-appraisal of shoulder position. Both groups responded similarly to active movements. Subjects with recurrent neck pain and control subjects showed similar trunk and head-to-trunk movements during shoulder movements. Subjects with recurrent neck pain, however, made larger movements compared with healthy subjects (p&lt;0.05). During trapezius muscle vibration similar whole-body movements were found in both groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These results show a modified interpretation of neck proprioceptive signals in subjects with recurrent neck pain and may reflect an offset in the egocentric reference frame or a decreased capacity to switch between reference frames. Better insight into these mechanisms might lead to better evaluation and treatment of subjects with recurrent neck pain and to a reduction in recurrent episodes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
